<?xml version="1.0" encoding="UTF-8"?>
<!DOCTYPE plist PUBLIC "-//Apple//DTD PLIST 1.0//EN" "http://www.apple.com/DTDs/PropertyList-1.0.dtd">
<!--
    Apple Privacy Manifest for Shipkit Pipes.

    Apple requires a privacy manifest and validates it at upload. All four
    required keys must be present even when empty, per Apple's spec:

      - NSPrivacyTracking                  Bool
      - NSPrivacyTrackingDomains           Array of String
      - NSPrivacyCollectedDataTypes        Array of dict
      - NSPrivacyAccessedAPITypes          Array of dict

    This app does no tracking and collects no data, so the first three stay
    empty — and test/privacy_manifest_test.rb asserts the collected-data array
    is empty BY NAME rather than letting an empty default pass unexamined.

    THE ONE DECLARED ENTRY, AND WHY IT LANDED IN THE COMMIT IT DID. Plan 07-05
    gave the app its first durable state: the last-used surface and four
    control values, behind a single seam in app/Shared/Model/AppModel.swift.
    That seam reaches a required-reason API, so the declaration below was
    written in the SAME commit as the code. A commit that added the code
    without the declaration would be a commit where the repository gate is
    legitimately red, and a manifest filled ahead of the code would be a
    declaration with nothing behind it.

    The reason code is CA92.1 — read and write information only accessible to
    the app itself. Deliberately NOT the App-Group-scoped reason (this app has
    no App Group), NOT the third-party-SDK-wrapper reason (this app has no
    third-party dependencies at all, by PROJECT.md constraint AR-05), and NOT
    the managed-configuration reason.

    OVER-DECLARING IS A FALSE STATEMENT, NOT A HARMLESS EXTRA. Apple's rule is
    that you may use these APIs, and the data derived from their use, for the
    DECLARED reasons only. So this list is asserted in BOTH directions by
    test/privacy_manifest_test.rb on every pull request: everything the app's
    own Swift reaches is declared here (ITMS-91053 rejects the gap), and
    everything declared here is reached by that Swift (an unused entry is the
    false statement). A declared entry with a missing or wrong-category reason
    is ITMS-91055.

    WHEN A FUTURE FEATURE NEEDS A SECOND ENTRY, look the categories up at the
    reference below, or read the run-time-assembled constants in
    test/privacy_manifest_test.rb. They are deliberately not named in this
    comment. This file is that gate's own subject, and until 07-05 the single
    occurrence of a category name anywhere under app/Shared/ was right here, in
    prose — a name that a file-wide scan would have had to report as a use on a
    correct tree and that, once this manifest was filled, could have satisfied
    a declaration no code justified. Shapes, not words.

    Edit this file STRUCTURALLY — plutil -insert … -json — never by text
    substitution. A sed on XML is how a manifest stops parsing.

    Reference: https://developer.apple.com/documentation/bundleresources/privacy_manifest_files
-->
<plist version="1.0">
<dict>
	<key>NSPrivacyAccessedAPITypes</key>
	<array>
		<dict>
			<key>NSPrivacyAccessedAPIType</key>
			<string>NSPrivacyAccessedAPICategoryUserDefaults</string>
			<key>NSPrivacyAccessedAPITypeReasons</key>
			<array>
				<string>CA92.1</string>
			</array>
		</dict>
	</array>
	<key>NSPrivacyCollectedDataTypes</key>
	<array/>
	<key>NSPrivacyTracking</key>
	<false/>
	<key>NSPrivacyTrackingDomains</key>
	<array/>
</dict>
</plist>
